{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_JSnhjLPlDxBRaH40bEByN0A6","content":"1\t# tricoach\n2\t\n3\tBackend + single-page app for a small triathlon coaching practice. Coaches\n4\tplan the week; athletes see it and connect their Intervals.icu account so the\n5\tapp can read wellness data and push planned workouts to their calendar. The\n6\tathlete signs in to tricoach (token in `localStorage`, sent as\n7\t`Authorization: Bearer` by the SPA's fetch wrapper), then clicks\n8\t\"Connect Intervals.icu\", which runs the OAuth flow against Intervals.\n9\t\n10\t`server.py` is stdlib only (`python3 server.py`, port 8766) and keeps\n11\teverything in memory; the shape is the same as the Postgres tables in prod.\n12\tDev sign-in tokens: `tok-okafor` (default in the SPA) and `tok-rivera`.\n13\t`config.py` holds the OAuth client placeholders. `logs/` has what we captured\n14\twhile looking into the connection problems."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_kZZCQ3mIPg6CM5cELiKhC411","content":"1\t### 1. An athlete clicks \"Connect Intervals.icu\" (server log, 2026-10-14)\n2\t\n3\t2026-10-14 18:02:11 [http] \"GET /api/me HTTP/1.1\" 200 -\n4\t2026-10-14 18:02:11 [http] \"GET /api/intervals/status HTTP/1.1\" 200 -\n5\t2026-10-14 18:02:19 [http] GET /api/intervals/auth/init 401 (no Authorization header)\n6\t2026-10-14 18:02:19 [http] \"GET /api/intervals/auth/init HTTP/1.1\" 401 -\n7\t\n8\t    Browser dev tools, same request (document navigation):\n9\t    Request URL:     http://localhost:8766/api/intervals/auth/init\n10\t    Request Method:  GET\n11\t    Status Code:     401 Unauthorized\n12\t    Request headers: Accept: text/html,application/xhtml+xml,... / Referer: http://localhost:8766/\n13\t    Response body:   {\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}\n14\t\n15\t### 2. Building the authorize link by hand and opening it (2026-10-17)\n16\t\n17\t$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{redirect_url}\\n' -H 'Authorization: Bearer tok-okafor' \\\n18\t    http://localhost:8766/api/intervals/auth/init\n19\thttps://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=tricoach-dev&redirect_uri=http:%2F%2Flocalhost:8766%2Fapi%2Fintervals%2Fcallback&scope=ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE&state=dV9va2Fmb3I6MTc5MjY4MTAwMDpiNjM1ZjBiZjU1M2ZjZWQ0YmM4ODNjMTExZmI4MDJhMw==\n20\t\n21\t$ curl -s -i \"<that url>\"\n22\tHTTP/2 400\n23\tcontent-type: application/json\n24\tcache-control: no-store\n25\t\n26\t{\"error\":\"invalid_request\"}\n27\t\n28\t    Same 400 in a browser (as the signed-in athlete on intervals.icu), no\n29\t    consent screen. Removing the state param or changing the redirect_uri\n30\t    gives the same body, so the body does not say which parameter it minds.\n31\t\n32\t### 3. The one connection that worked: T. Rivera, onboarding call (2026-09-19)\n33\t\n34\t2026-09-19 17:41:03 [http] \"GET /api/intervals/callback?code=a7Jk2...&state=dV9yaXZlcmE6... HTTP/1.1\" 302 -\n35\t2026-09-19 17:41:04 [intervals] token response from https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token:\n36\t{\"token_type\":\"bearer\",\"access_token\":\"9f3c1e0b4d7a...redacted...\",\"scope\":\"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ\",\"athlete\":{\"id\":\"i98765\",\"name\":\"T. Rivera\"}}\n37\t2026-09-19 17:41:04 [intervals] u_rivera: no refresh_token in response, scheduling expiry in 3600s\n38\t2026-09-19 17:41:04 [intervals] u_rivera: connected as athlete i98765 (expires in 3600s)\n39\t2026-09-19 17:44:30 [http] \"GET /api/intervals/wellness HTTP/1.1\" 200 -\n40\t2026-09-19 18:37:12 [intervals] u_rivera: token expiry passed and no refresh token on file -> expired, reconnect\n41\t2026-09-20 07:12:48 [http] \"GET /api/intervals/status HTTP/1.1\" 200 -"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_jT4o7rmo49wNVValhPBF1XRs","content":"1\t# Support tickets — Intervals.icu connection\n2\t\n3\t## #214 — \"Connect Intervals.icu shows Unauthorized\" (C. Okafor, 2026-10-14)\n4\t\n5\t> I'm signed in, the page says \"Signed in as C. Okafor\", I click Connect\n6\t> Intervals.icu and get a white page that just says\n7\t> `{\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}`. Back button, try again, same thing. Tried\n8\t> Safari and Chrome.\n9\t\n10\tReproduced with my own account. Same for the four athletes onboarded this\n11\tweek; every athlete who clicks Connect ends up on that page. Nobody has\n12\tgotten through the button since we started onboarding athletes.\n13\t\n14\t## #219 — \"Says I need to reconnect\" (T. Rivera, 2026-10-16)\n15\t\n16\t> My Intervals was connected during the onboarding call last month and\n17\t> wellness showed up fine that evening. Next morning the card said\n18\t> \"Status: expired, reconnect\". I clicked Connect and got the same\n19\t> unauthorized page as Chloe. I did NOT revoke anything on the Intervals\n20\t> side — tricoach is still listed under my Intervals apps.\n21\t\n22\tChecked our logs for the onboarding day: Rivera's connection was marked\n23\t\"expired, reconnect\" about an hour after it was made (see\n24\t`connect-attempt.log`). Rivera was connected by me pasting the authorize\n25\tlink into their browser during the call, which is how I got around the\n26\tbutton. Pasting the link does not work anymore either: since the calendar\n27\tsync shipped on 2026-10-02 the integration also asks for calendar access, and\n28\tIntervals now answers the authorize step with an error page before the\n29\tathlete ever sees the consent screen (captured below in\n30\t`connect-attempt.log`)."}
{"role":"assistant","content":"Now let me look at the server code and the SPA:","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_ex8AAgFxM5YmcvwJakcZSHrS","function":{"name":"Read","arguments":"{\"path\":\"server.py\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_BwKUj7cPyKNqRrMCt2rdgpTH","function":{"name":"Read","arguments":"{\"path\":\"config.py\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_gRs1pA8cPTuz5OGNpQ558pXT","function":{"name":"Read","arguments":"{\"path\":\"static/app.js\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_BwKUj7cPyKNqRrMCt2rdgpTH","content":"1\timport os\n2\t\n3\tPORT = 8766\n4\tAPP_SECRET = \"dev-only-change-me-7c1f0a\"          # signs the OAuth state parameter\n5\t\n6\t# Intervals.icu OAuth client (placeholders; the real pair is in the deploy env)\n7\tINTERVALS_BASE = os.environ.get(\"INTERVALS_BASE\", \"https://intervals.icu\")\n8\tCLIENT_ID = \"tricoach-dev\"\n9\tCLIENT_SECRET = \"s3cr3t-placeholder-0000\"\n10\tREDIRECT_URI = f\"http://localhost:{PORT}/api/intervals/callback\"\n11\tSCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE\"\n12\t\n13\tREFRESH_INTERVAL = 60      # seconds between passes of the token refresher"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_ex8AAgFxM5YmcvwJakcZSHrS","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"tricoach backend: sign-in, Intervals.icu OAuth connection, wellness proxy.\n3\t\n4\tStdlib only. State is in memory (same shape as the prod tables).\n5\t\"\"\"\n6\timport base64, hashlib, hmac, json, os, threading, time\n7\timport urllib.error, urllib.parse, urllib.request\n8\tfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer\n9\t\n10\timport config\n11\t\n12\tSTATIC = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), \"static\")\n13\tTOKEN_URL = config.INTERVALS_BASE + \"/api/oauth/token\"\n14\tAUTHORIZE_URL = config.INTERVALS_BASE + \"/oauth/authorize\"\n15\tDEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL = 3600   # seconds; used when the provider does not say how long a token lives\n16\t\n17\t# --- in-memory store -------------------------------------------------------\n18\tUSERS = {\n19\t    \"tok-okafor\": {\"id\": \"u_okafor\", \"name\": \"C. Okafor\"},\n20\t    \"tok-rivera\": {\"id\": \"u_rivera\", \"name\": \"T. Rivera\"},\n21\t}\n22\t# user_id -> {athlete_id, athlete_name, access_token, refresh_token, scope, expires_at, status}\n23\tCONNECTIONS = {}\n24\tLOCK = threading.Lock()\n25\t\n26\t\n27\tdef log(msg):\n28\t    print(time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\"), \"[intervals]\", msg, flush=True)\n29\t\n30\t\n31\t# --- state parameter: carries the user through the redirect -----------------\n32\tdef sign_state(user_id):\n33\t    payload = f\"{user_id}:{int(time.time())}\"\n34\t    sig = hmac.new(config.APP_SECRET.encode(), payload.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()[:32]\n35\t    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(f\"{payload}:{sig}\".encode()).decode()\n36\t\n37\t\n38\tdef verify_state(state, max_age=900):\n39\t    try:\n40\t        user_id, ts, sig = base64.urlsafe_b64decode(state.encode()).decode().split(\":\")\n41\t    except Exception:\n42\t        return None\n43\t    payload = f\"{user_id}:{ts}\"\n44\t    good = hmac.new(config.APP_SECRET.encode(), payload.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()[:32]\n45\t    if not hmac.compare_digest(good, sig) or time.time() - int(ts) > max_age:\n46\t        return None\n47\t    return user_id\n48\t\n49\t\n50\t# --- Intervals.icu calls ----------------------------------------------------\n51\tdef post_form(url, fields):\n52\t    data = urllib.parse.urlencode(fields).encode()\n53\t    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=data, headers={\"Accept\": \"application/json\"})\n54\t    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=15) as r:\n55\t        return json.loads(r.read().decode())\n56\t\n57\t\n58\tdef exchange_code(code):\n59\t    return post_form(TOKEN_URL, {\n60\t        \"grant_type\": \"authorization_code\", \"client_id\": config.CLIENT_ID,\n61\t        \"client_secret\": config.CLIENT_SECRET, \"code\": code, \"redirect_uri\": config.REDIRECT_URI,\n62\t    })\n63\t\n64\t\n65\tdef store_connection(user_id, tok):\n66\t    expires_in = int(tok.get(\"expires_in\") or DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL)\n67\t    refresh = tok.get(\"refresh_token\")\n68\t    if not refresh:\n69\t        log(f\"{user_id}: no refresh_token in response, scheduling expiry in {expires_in}s\")\n70\t    athlete = tok.get(\"athlete\") or {}\n71\t    with LOCK:\n72\t        CONNECTIONS[user_id] = {\n73\t            \"athlete_id\": athlete.get(\"id\"), \"athlete_name\": athlete.get(\"name\"),\n74\t            \"access_token\": tok[\"access_token\"], \"refresh_token\": refresh,\n75\t            \"scope\": tok.get(\"scope\"), \"expires_at\": time.time() + expires_in, \"status\": \"connected\",\n76\t        }\n77\t    log(f\"{user_id}: connected as athlete {athlete.get('id')} (expires in {expires_in}s)\")\n78\t\n79\t\n80\tdef refresh_tokens():\n81\t    \"\"\"Background pass: renew access tokens shortly before they expire.\"\"\"\n82\t    while True:\n83\t        time.sleep(config.REFRESH_INTERVAL)\n84\t        now = time.time()\n85\t        with LOCK:\n86\t            for uid, c in CONNECTIONS.items():\n87\t                if c[\"status\"] != \"connected\" or c[\"expires_at\"] - now > 300:\n88\t                    continue\n89\t                if not c[\"refresh_token\"]:\n90\t                    log(f\"{uid}: token expiry passed and no refresh token on file -> expired, reconnect\")\n91\t                    c.update(status=\"expired, reconnect\", access_token=None)\n92\t                    continue\n93\t                try:\n94\t                    tok = post_form(TOKEN_URL, {\n95\t                        \"grant_type\": \"refresh_token\", \"refresh_token\": c[\"refresh_token\"],\n96\t                        \"client_id\": config.CLIENT_ID, \"client_secret\": config.CLIENT_SECRET,\n97\t                    })\n98\t                    c.update(access_token=tok[\"access_token\"],\n99\t                             refresh_token=tok.get(\"refresh_token\", c[\"refresh_token\"]),\n100\t                             expires_at=now + int(tok[\"expires_in\"]))\n101\t                    log(f\"{uid}: token refreshed\")\n102\t                except Exception as e:\n103\t                    log(f\"{uid}: refresh failed ({e}) -> expired, reconnect\")\n104\t                    c.update(status=\"expired, reconnect\", access_token=None)\n105\t\n106\t\n107\tdef intervals_get(conn, path):\n108\t    req = urllib.request.Request(f\"{config.INTERVALS_BASE}/api/v1/athlete/{conn['athlete_id']}{path}\",\n109\t                                 headers={\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + conn[\"access_token\"]})\n110\t    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=15) as r:\n111\t        return r.status, json.loads(r.read().decode())\n112\t\n113\t\n114\t# --- HTTP ---------------------------------------------------------------------\n115\tclass Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n116\t    def log_message(self, fmt, *args):\n117\t        print(time.strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\"), \"[http]\", fmt % args, flush=True)\n118\t\n119\t    def send_json(self, code, obj):\n120\t        body = json.dumps(obj).encode()\n121\t        self.send_response(code)\n122\t        self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n123\t        self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(body)))\n124\t        self.end_headers()\n125\t        self.wfile.write(body)\n126\t\n127\t    def send_file(self, name, ctype):\n128\t        p = os.path.join(STATIC, name)\n129\t        if not os.path.isfile(p):\n130\t            return self.send_json(404, {\"error\": \"not found\"})\n131\t        with open(p, \"rb\") as f:\n132\t            body = f.read()\n133\t        self.send_response(200)\n134\t        self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", ctype)\n135\t        self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(body)))\n136\t        self.end_headers()\n137\t        self.wfile.write(body)\n138\t\n139\t    def redirect(self, url):\n140\t        self.send_response(302)\n141\t        self.send_header(\"Location\", url)\n142\t        self.end_headers()\n143\t\n144\t    def current_user(self):\n145\t        auth = self.headers.get(\"Authorization\", \"\")\n146\t        if not auth.startswith(\"Bearer \"):\n147\t            return None\n148\t        return USERS.get(auth[7:].strip())\n149\t\n150\t    def require_user(self):\n151\t        user = self.current_user()\n152\t        if user is None:\n153\t            self.log_message(\"%s %s 401 (no Authorization header)\", self.command, self.path)\n154\t            self.send_json(401, {\"error\": \"unauthorized\"})\n155\t        return user\n156\t\n157\t    def do_GET(self):\n158\t        url = urllib.parse.urlsplit(self.path)\n159\t        q = dict(urllib.parse.parse_qsl(url.query))\n160\t        if url.path == \"/\":\n161\t            return self.send_file(\"index.html\", \"text/html; charset=utf-8\")\n162\t        if url.path.startswith(\"/static/\"):\n163\t            return self.send_file(os.path.basename(url.path), \"application/javascript\" if url.path.endswith(\".js\") else \"text/plain\")\n164\t        if url.path == \"/api/me\":\n165\t            user = self.require_user()\n166\t            return user and self.send_json(200, user)\n167\t        if url.path == \"/api/intervals/auth/init\":\n168\t            return self.auth_init()\n169\t        if url.path == \"/api/intervals/callback\":\n170\t            return self.callback(q)\n171\t        if url.path == \"/api/intervals/status\":\n172\t            return self.status()\n173\t        if url.path == \"/api/intervals/wellness\":\n174\t            return self.wellness()\n175\t        self.send_json(404, {\"error\": \"not found\"})\n176\t\n177\t    def do_POST(self):\n178\t        url = urllib.parse.urlsplit(self.path)\n179\t        if url.path == \"/api/intervals/disconnect\":\n180\t            user = self.require_user()\n181\t            if user:\n182\t                with LOCK:\n183\t                    CONNECTIONS.pop(user[\"id\"], None)\n184\t                self.send_json(200, {\"ok\": True})\n185\t            return\n186\t        self.send_json(404, {\"error\": \"not found\"})\n187\t\n188\t    # Step 1: build the authorize URL and send the browser to Intervals.\n189\t    def auth_init(self):\n190\t        user = self.require_user()\n191\t        if not user:\n192\t            return\n193\t        params = {\"client_id\": config.CLIENT_ID, \"redirect_uri\": config.REDIRECT_URI,\n194\t                  \"scope\": config.SCOPES, \"state\": sign_state(user[\"id\"])}\n195\t        self.redirect(AUTHORIZE_URL + \"?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode(params, safe=\":,\"))\n196\t\n197\t    # Step 2: Intervals redirects back here with ?code=&state=.\n198\t    def callback(self, q):\n199\t        user_id = verify_state(q.get(\"state\", \"\"))\n200\t        if not user_id or not q.get(\"code\"):\n201\t            return self.send_json(400, {\"error\": \"bad state or code\"})\n202\t        try:\n203\t            tok = exchange_code(q[\"code\"])\n204\t            store_connection(user_id, tok)\n205\t        except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:\n206\t            log(f\"{user_id}: token exchange failed: HTTP {e.code} {e.read()[:200]!r}\")\n207\t            return self.send_json(502, {\"error\": \"token exchange failed\"})\n208\t        except Exception as e:\n209\t            log(f\"{user_id}: token exchange failed: {e}\")\n210\t            return self.send_json(502, {\"error\": \"token exchange failed\"})\n211\t        self.redirect(\"/?connected=1\")\n212\t\n213\t    def status(self):\n214\t        user = self.require_user()\n215\t        if not user:\n216\t            return\n217\t        with LOCK:\n218\t            c = CONNECTIONS.get(user[\"id\"])\n219\t        if not c or c[\"status\"] != \"connected\":\n220\t            return self.send_json(200, {\"connected\": False, \"status\": c[\"status\"] if c else \"disconnected\"})\n221\t        self.send_json(200, {\"connected\": True, \"status\": \"connected\", \"scope\": c[\"scope\"],\n222\t                             \"athlete\": {\"id\": c[\"athlete_id\"], \"name\": c[\"athlete_name\"]}})\n223\t\n224\t    def wellness(self):\n225\t        user = self.require_user()\n226\t        if not user:\n227\t            return\n228\t        with LOCK:\n229\t            c = CONNECTIONS.get(user[\"id\"])\n230\t        if not c or c[\"status\"] != \"connected\":\n231\t            return self.send_json(409, {\"error\": \"intervals not connected\"})\n232\t        try:\n233\t            code, data = intervals_get(c, \"/wellness\")\n234\t            self.send_json(code, data)\n235\t        except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:\n236\t            self.send_json(e.code, {\"error\": f\"intervals returned {e.code}\"})\n237\t        except Exception as e:\n238\t            self.send_json(502, {\"error\": str(e)})\n239\t\n240\t\n241\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n242\t    threading.Thread(target=refresh_tokens, daemon=True).start()\n243\t    log(f\"serving on http://localhost:{config.PORT} (intervals: {config.INTERVALS_BASE})\")\n244\t    ThreadingHTTPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", config.PORT), Handler).serve_forever()"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_gRs1pA8cPTuz5OGNpQ558pXT","content":"1\t// tricoach SPA. The sign-in token lives in localStorage and travels on every\n2\t// API call via the fetch wrapper below.\n3\tconst TOKEN = localStorage.getItem(\"tc_token\") || \"tok-okafor\";\n4\tconst root = document.getElementById(\"app\");\n5\t\n6\tasync function api(path, opts = {}) {\n7\t  const headers = Object.assign({ \"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + TOKEN }, opts.headers || {});\n8\t  const res = await fetch(path, Object.assign({}, opts, { headers }));\n9\t  const body = await res.json().catch(() => ({}));\n10\t  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(body.error || (\"HTTP \" + res.status));\n11\t  return body;\n12\t}\n13\t\n14\tfunction esc(s) { return String(s).replace(/[&<>\"]/g, c => ({ \"&\": \"&amp;\", \"<\": \"&lt;\", \">\": \"&gt;\", '\"': \"&quot;\" }[c])); }\n15\t\n16\tasync function render() {\n17\t  try {\n18\t    const me = await api(\"/api/me\");\n19\t    const st = await api(\"/api/intervals/status\");\n20\t    let card;\n21\t    if (st.connected) {\n22\t      card = `<div class=\"card\">Connected to Intervals.icu as <b>${esc(st.athlete.name)}</b>\n23\t        (${esc(st.athlete.id)}), scope <code>${esc(st.scope)}</code>.\n24\t        <p><button class=\"btn\" id=\"disconnect\">Disconnect</button></p></div>`;\n25\t    } else {\n26\t      const why = st.status && st.status !== \"disconnected\" ? `<p class=\"muted\">Status: ${esc(st.status)}</p>` : \"\";\n27\t      card = `<div class=\"card\">Intervals.icu is not connected.${why}\n28\t        <p><a class=\"btn\" href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">Connect Intervals.icu</a></p></div>`;\n29\t    }\n30\t    root.className = \"\";\n31\t    root.innerHTML = `<p>Signed in as <b>${esc(me.name)}</b>.</p>${card}`;\n32\t    const d = document.getElementById(\"disconnect\");\n33\t    if (d) d.onclick = async () => { await api(\"/api/intervals/disconnect\", { method: \"POST\" }); render(); };\n34\t  } catch (e) {\n35\t    root.innerHTML = `<p class=\"card\">Error: ${esc(e.message)}</p>`;\n36\t  }\n37\t}\n38\t\n39\trender();"}
{"role":"assistant","content":"I have a good picture of the app now. Three distinct symptoms:\n\n1. **Button 401s for everyone** — the Connect control is a plain `<a href>` navigation, which can't carry the `Authorization` header that `require_user` demands (the dev-tools capture in the log confirms no auth header on the navigation).\n2. **Authorize URL 400s** — since the calendar sync shipped and added `CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` to the scope list, Intervals rejects the authorize request before consent.\n3. **Rivera's token \"expired\" after an hour with no refresh token** — the raw token response in the log has no `refresh_token` and no `expires_in`, and the server assumed a 3600 s TTL.\n\nBefore fixing, let me confirm the real Intervals.icu OAuth contract (valid scopes, token response shape, expiry semantics) from their docs:","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_0P3UZIYYHC4uRbGkLeos4fIl","function":{"name":"WebSearch","arguments":"{\"query\":\"Intervals.icu API OAuth documentation scopes refresh_token authorize\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_NstOrPldfd9KCHZiDAeIecIO","function":{"name":"WebSearch","arguments":"{\"query\":\"intervals.icu \\\"api/oauth/token\\\" \\\"refresh_token\\\" \\\"expires_in\\\"\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_0P3UZIYYHC4uRbGkLeos4fIl","content":"Title: kuranov/intervals-icu-tools\nSite: Github\nDate: 2024-01-01\nURL: https://github.com/kuranov/intervals-icu-tools\nSnippet: Supports both API key and OAuth2 authentication. The library handles token management and refresh. OAuth2 flow requires separate handling of authorization URL ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: issue: OAuth scopes are missing within authorize_url when using MCP with OAuth2.1 (Static)\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-05-14\nURL: https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui/issues/24730\nSnippet: Expected Behavior Openwebui should be able to retrieve scopes from `.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` and include those scopes when generating the `authorize` endpoint. Actual Behavior When openwebui try to generate authorization url, it missing scopes because of this line [1] Generate URL: ```notranslate https://login.microsoftonline.com/organizations/v2.0/authorize?response_type=code&client_id=&redirect_uri=&resource=https%3A%2F%2Fagent365.svc.cloud.microsoft%2Fagents%2Ftenants%2Ff%2Fserve\n\n---\n\nTitle: issue: OAuth scopes are still missing within authorize_url when using MCP with OAuth2.1 (Static)\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-06-10\nURL: https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui/issues/25898\nSnippet: Verification Logs & Screenshots Error, when activating the MCP Server: Additional Information - Related: PR fix(oauth): use Protected Resource Metadata scopes in static OAuth 2.1 flow #24690 fixed the scope discovery in get_oauth_client_info_with_static_credentials but did not address the persistence gap where the scope is lost between build and runtime load - Related: Issue issue: OAuth scopes are missing within authorize_url when using MCP with OAuth2.1 (Static) #24730 was closed as fixed by P\n\n---\n\nTitle: What Are Refresh Tokens and How to Use Them Securely\nSite: Auth0\nDate: 2015-10-07\nURL: https://auth0.com/blog/refresh-tokens-what-are-they-and-when-to-use-them/\nSnippet: Learn more about refresh tokens and how they help developers balance security, privacy, and usability in their applications.\n\n---\n\nTitle: OAuth 2.0 - Integrations - Zoom Developer Docs\nSite: zoom.us\nDate: 2019-04-05\nURL: https://developers.zoom.us/docs/integrations/oauth/\nSnippet: Learn how to request, refresh, and revoke OAuth 2.0 access tokens for Zoom APIs across four authorization flows. Tokens expire after a set time and may need ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: README.md · mirrors_ballerina-platform/module-ballerinax-hubspot.crm.commerce.discounts - Gitee.com\nSite: Gitee\nDate: 2025-01-23\nURL: https://gitee.com/mirrors_ballerina-platform/module-ballerinax-hubspot.crm.commerce.discounts/blob/main/README.md?skip_mobile=true\nSnippet: Ballerina HubSpot CRM Commerce Discounts connector Overview HubSpot is an AI-powered customer relationship management (CRM) platform. The `ballerinax/hubspot.crm.commerce.discounts` connector offers APIs to connect and interact with the HubSpot Discounts API endpoints, specifically based on the HubSpot REST API. Setup guide You need a HubSpot developer account with an app to use HubSpot connectors. > To create a HubSpot Developer account, click here Step 1: Create HubSpot Developer Project 1. Lo\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Announcements\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759\nSnippet: OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access an Intervals.icu user's data. It allows users to grant and revoke API ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu - Page 8 - Guide\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2020-04-01\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=8\nSnippet: Intervals.icu now has a public API for other applications. To use it you need to generate an API key in /settings (look for “Developer ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Use OAuth 2.0 Scopes to Authorize API Actions\nSite: sergiodxa.com\nDate: 2025-03-24\nURL: https://sergiodxa.com/tutorials/use-scope-to-authorize-actions-in-your-api\nSnippet: When using OAuth2 with access tokens, one of the most important claims is `scope`. The `scope` claim defines what the token is allowed to do. Each scope represents a permission—like `contacts.read`, `contacts.write`, or `calendar.edit`. Instead of relying on roles or user types, scopes give you a more granular way to control access in your API. Example: Enforcing `contacts.read` scope If your API receives a JWT, you can use the `scope` claim to check if the client has permission to access a spec\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu API Integration Cookbook - Guide\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2024-11-20\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-api-integration-cookbook/80090\nSnippet: This tells you everything you need to know to get completed activities into and out of Intervals.icu, to download planned workouts and to read ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Page 2 - Announcements\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759?page=2\nSnippet: Intervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access an ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Page 3\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759?page=3\nSnippet: Intervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_NstOrPldfd9KCHZiDAeIecIO","content":"Title: [BUG] MCP OAuth tokens without expires_in and refresh_token silently expires\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-02-17\nURL: https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/26281\nSnippet: What's Wrong? When an MCP server's OAuth token exchange endpoint `/oauth/token` returns only an `access_token` without `expires_in` or `refresh_token` (e.g. GitHub OAuth tokens), Claude Code silently expires the credential after some time (more than 30 minutes but less than 24 hours) and requires re-authentication. After the token expires, Claude Code attempts the MCP `initialize` request without the Authorization header, which fails. The user must then manually re-authenticate via `/mcp`. The t\n\n---\n\nTitle: banshu-ai 1.1.0\nSite: Docs.rs\nDate: 2026-11-06\nURL: https://docs.rs/crate/banshu-ai/latest/source/src/kimi.rs\nSnippet: ... api/oauth/token` for both the device-code ... expires_in: Option, interval: Option ... refresh_token\", refresh_token.as_str()), ]) .send ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Google OAuth Refresh Token Expires After 1 Hour (PHP Google Ads API with Laravel)\nSite: Stack Overflow\nDate: 2024-04-19\nURL: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78353257/google-oauth-refresh-token-expires-after-1-hour-php-google-ads-api-with-laravel\nSnippet: My Google OAuth refresh token expires after one hour, even though both my OAuth and Google Ads API applications are verified and approved. - Both OAuth and Google Ads API apps are verified and approved. - Created a new client_credentials.json file after approval. - Using the provided Laravel example for the PHP Google Ads API. - Added the developer token with basic access and populated clientId and clientSecret in the google_ads_php.ini file. For the refresh token i used command ~/go/bin/oauth2l\n\n---\n\nTitle: Hubspot Trigger has no Refresh Token, token expires - Questions - n8n Community\nSite: n8n\nDate: 2025-04-17\nURL: https://community.n8n.io/t/hubspot-trigger-has-no-refresh-token-token-expires/102428\nSnippet: post by Saikalyan_Akunuri on Apr 18, 2025 Have you included offline scope in your hubspot account. HubSpot only issues refresh tokens if you include the `offline` scope when authenticating. The documentation is a bit vague but I think that could be the issue. HubSpot Developer Documentation \" If you need offline access to HubSpot data, store the refresh token you get when initiating your OAuth integration and use it to generate a new access token once the initial one expires.\" post by MEmke on A\n\n---\n\nTitle: How can I auto refresh OAuth 2.0 token once the token expires.? - Help Hub - Postman Community\nSite: Postman\nDate: 2021-02-05\nURL: https://community.postman.com/t/how-can-i-auto-refresh-oauth-2-0-token-once-the-token-expires/20062\nSnippet: post by pranavNathcorp on Feb 5, 2021 How can I auto refresh OAuth 2.0 token once the token expires. ? post by swapnil.gore on Feb 5, 2021 Hi @pranavNathcorp Welcome to Postman Community! At this point, it isn’t possible to auto-refresh the oAuth2.0 token, and we are having this as a feature request which is being tracked on our GitHub page, and our engineering team is working on it. Please subscribe to it to get updates and do provide your comments/feedback directly there. Hope this helps! post\n\n---\n\nTitle: What is the correct way to refresh Facebook OAuth2 access token after it expires?\nSite: exchangetuts.com\nURL: https://exchangetuts.com/what-is-the-correct-way-to-refresh-facebook-oauth2-access-token-after-it-expires-1639579509265084\nSnippet: People also ask How do I refresh my Facebook access token? These tokens are refreshed once per day, when the person using your app makes a request to Facebook's servers. If no requests are made, the token will expire after about 60 days and the person will have to go through the login flow again to get a new token. How do I refresh OAuth access token? To use the refresh token, make a POST request to the service's token endpoint with grant_type=refresh_token , and include the refresh token as wel\n\n---\n\nTitle: Access Token vs Refresh Token: What Really Happens When Your Login \"Expires\"\nSite: OrbitTest\nDate: 2026-07-24\nURL: https://www.orbittest.dev/blog/access-token-vs-refresh-token-explained\nSnippet: Conclusion There’s no magic keeping you logged in for days at a time. There’s a short-lived access token quietly expiring every few minutes, a `401` response that never reaches your screen, and a refresh token working in the background to get you a new one — right up until it, too, eventually expires and asks you to log in for real. Understanding that cycle changes how you think about “what if a token leaks.” The honest answer isn’t “it’s impossible” — it’s that the system is deliberately built \n\n---\n\nTitle: OAuth and OpenID Connect | Chief Tools | Chief Tools Documentation\nSite: chief.tools\nURL: https://docs.chief.tools/developers/discovery\nSnippet: POST /api/oauth/token HTTP/1.1 Host: account ... Use expires_in to schedule renewal. Do not decode ... application/x-www-form-urlencoded grant_type= ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: What happens if my OAuth token expires?\nSite: frejun.com\nURL: https://knowledge.frejun.com/what-happens-if-my-oauth-token-expires\nSnippet: OAuth tokens play a critical role in authenticating API and SDK requests within FreJun. When an OAuth token expires, your application may no longer be authorized to access FreJun services, leading to failed requests or unexpected behavior. Understanding how token expiration works helps you maintain uninterrupted API communication and system reliability. In this article, we explain what happens when your OAuth token expires, how it affects FreJun API and SDK requests, common error responses like \n\n---\n\nTitle: Salesforce: How to get the access token expires_at\nSite: Nango Docs\nDate: 2025-07-24\nURL: https://nango.dev/blog/salesforce-get-access-token-expires-at/\nSnippet: Salesforce breaks with a common OAuth convention: Their token endpoint does not return the `expires_at` parameter, which indicates when the access token expires. In this post we will show you how to retrieve the expiration date of Salesforce access tokens. Problem: No expires_at in token response A typical response from Salesforce’s access token endpoint looks like this: ``` { \"access_token\":\"00Dx00...\", \"refresh_token\":\"CjA...\", \"signature\":\"SSSbLO/gBhmmyNUvN18ODBDFYHzakxOMgqYtu+hDPsc=\", \"scope\n\n---\n\nTitle: Authentication & OAuth\nSite: Netmon\nURL: https://netmon.com/api-reference/authentication/\nSnippet: See /api/oauth/token/refresh . Response 200 { \"access_token\": \"eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOi…\", \"token_type\": \"Bearer\", \"expires_in\": 3600, \"refresh_token\": \"…\n\n---\n\nTitle: Device Authorization Flow\nSite: storyden.org\nURL: https://www.storyden.org/docs/introduction/oauth/device-flow\nSnippet: ... api/oauth/token\" ` -ContentType \"application/x ... refresh_token grant: { \"access_token\": \"eyJ...\", \"token_type\": \"Bearer\", \"expires_in ... Increase interval and ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Agent Account Signup API\nSite: ReelsMeals\nURL: https://reelsmeals.com/agents/signup\nSnippet: After approval the token response contains access_token , refresh_token , expires_in and granted scope . Send the access token as a Bearer credential to ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Cancellation of transaction - GP tom\nSite: gptom.com\nDate: 2020-09-16\nURL: https://www.gptom.com/en/docs/api/cloudapi/storno-transakce/\nSnippet: ... refresh_token\": \"GciO...\", \"expires_in\": 3600, \"scope\": \"read write ... POST {{apiHost}}/api/oauth/token. Authorization: Basic ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Auth Token \"expires_in\" meaning? - General - Node-RED Forum\nSite: Node-RED Forum\nDate: 2022-01-28\nURL: https://discourse.nodered.org/t/auth-token-expires-in-meaning/57377\nSnippet: TotallyInformation kuema Hi nodered experts, May I know the meaning for the expires_in in the admin API of nodered? I believed this is the range of time where the token will be expire, but I am not sure wether it is in minutes? hour? or days? The 604800 means? image In addition, I want my token to never get expired, is it possible? Regards, Henjoe TotallyInformation kuema That value looks familiar, so my _guess_ is, those are seconds. Meaning `604800 = 60 * 60 * 24 * 7`, which is one week. I see\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
{"role":"assistant","content":"Let me go straight to the Intervals.icu OAuth announcement and their API docs:","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_Aku9Q0WnBAvuCRz49095cgp2","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_l2ZGFgd3KRp9TMtwFoGhLXEj","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://intervals.icu/api/v1/swagger.json\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_Aku9Q0WnBAvuCRz49095cgp2","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\nIntervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access an Intervals.icu user’s data. It allows users to grant and revoke API access on a per-application basis and keeps user authentication details safe.\n\n## Heading link Creating An Application\n\nFill in the application form here: https://intervals.icu/oauth/apply\n\nYou need to be logged in as the Intervals.icu athlete that will own the app. This athlete will have access to the management page to edit the app, setup redirect URI’s, webhooks and other things.\n\nYour app will show up on the /settings/apps page (only for you as the owner) and you can click “Manage App” to retrieve your client\\_id and secret, change redirect URL’s, configure webhooks and so on.\n\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/cb935eaa9a1b9e784347f5a9486fe24a.png)\n\nThe app will have status “Pending” until it is approved. **Please note that you will not be able to do the OAuth flow until it is approved**. You can upload a logo, get the client ID and secret and so on.\n\nOnce it has been approved, the pending tag will disappear. You will receive a system notification when this happens (delivered by email and in the Intervals.icu app).\n\nNote that it will not be visible to other users in /settings/apps, but you can send anyone you like to the OAuth consent page and begin integration.\n\n\n![image2](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/ef7703e10049bf07ae1b507e4796aacc.png)\n\nWhen you have completed your integration and tested properly, contact support@intervals.icu to have it listed in the public app directory. Please include a link to the management page in the email.\n\n## Heading link Requesting Authorization To Access Intervals.icu Data For A User\n\nSend the user to:\n\n```lang-auto\nhttps://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=<your client id>\n    &redirect_uri=<your redirect uri>\n    &scope=<required scopes>\n    &state=<optional data>\n\n```\n\nIntervals.icu will ask the user to login and display a confirmation dialog with options to choose which\n\nscopes to grant the application. If the user confirms then they are redirected to the redirect\\_uri with an\n\nauthorization code and the optional state parameter:\n\n```lang-auto\n<your redirect uri>?code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4&state=...\n\n```\n\nIf they decline:\n\n```lang-auto\n<your redirect uri>?error=access_denied\n\n```\n\nYour server needs to exchange the code for an access token within 2 minutes by POSTing form data including your client\\_id and client\\_secret:\n\n```lang-auto\ncurl -X POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token \\\n    -d client_id=... \\\n    -d client_secret=... \\\n    -d code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4\n\n```\n\nIf all goes well Intervals.icu will respond with an access token, granted scopes and the id and name of the athlete:\n\n```lang-auto\n{\n    \"token_type\": \"Bearer\",\n    \"access_token\": \"d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9\",\n    \"scope\": \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:WRITE\",\n    \"athlete\": {\n        \"id\": \"2049151\",\n        \"name\": \"David (intervals.icu)\"\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\nTo call the API use “Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9” header.\n\nNote that you can use “0” for the athlete id for endpoints that accept an athlete id in the path. This will use the athlete for the bearer token used to make the call.\n\n### Heading link PKCE (optional)\n\nPKCE is supported to protect the authorization code from interception. Generate a random `code_verifier` (43-128 chars of `A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~`) and add its challenge to the authorize URL (only `S256` is supported):\n\n```\n    &code_challenge=<BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier)) without padding>\n    &code_challenge_method=S256\n\n```\n\nThen include `-d code_verifier=...` when exchanging the code. If a challenge was sent, the verifier is required, and a verifier is rejected if no challenge was sent. The client\\_secret is still required unless your app is a public client.\n\nOnce every version of your app uses PKCE, tick “Require PKCE” on your app’s settings page. Authorizations without a code\\_challenge are then rejected, and so is exchanging a code without a code\\_verifier, even with the client\\_secret.\n\n### Heading link Public clients (SPA, mobile and desktop apps)\n\nApps that cannot keep a client\\_secret confidential can be flagged as public clients on the OAuth app management page. Public clients that use PKCE can omit client\\_secret when exchanging the code. Without PKCE the client\\_secret is still required (this is supported so existing apps can migrate, please switch to PKCE). The automatic http://localhost redirect exception does not apply to public clients, so register the exact redirect URIs you use, including any localhost ones. The port of a registered http://localhost or http://127.0.0.1 redirect is ignored, so native apps can listen on any free port:\n\n```\ncurl -X POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token \\\n    -d client_id=...\n    -d code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4\n    -d code_verifier=...\n\n```\n\n## Heading link Scopes\n\nScopes are as follows:\n\n- ACTIVITY: Completed rides, runs etc.\n- WELLNESS: Weight, resting HR etc.\n- CALENDAR: Planned workouts\n- CHATS: Chats, groups and messages\n- LIBRARY: Workout library\n- SETTINGS: Athlete settings\n\nFor each scope specify READ or WRITE (to update, implies READ access) and use commas to separate multiple scopes. Example:\n\n```lang-auto\nACTIVITY:READ,WELLNESS:WRITE\n\n```\n\nRequests read access to activities and read and write access to wellness data.\n\n## Heading link Revoking An Access Token\n\nWhen a user disconnects Intervals.icu in your app or deletes their data on your side, revoke the access token:\n\n```lang-auto\ncurl -X DELETE \"https://intervals.icu/api/v1/disconnect-app\" \\\n -H 'authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThis will stop webhook delivery for the athlete.\n\n## Heading link Your Own Data\n\nNote that you don’t need to do all this if you just want access to your own data. Use your API key to do that.\n\n## Heading link API Endpoints"}
