{"role":"assistant","content":"Now the code fixes. First `server.py` — the `auth/init` endpoint must return the authorize URL as JSON (so the SPA can fetch it with the bearer token, then navigate):","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_BcHTE32IPFDl8d7wJQaUbDEm","function":{"name":"Edit","arguments":"{\"path\":\"server.py\",\"old_string\":\"    # Step 1: build the authorize URL and send the browser to Intervals.\\n    def auth_init(self):\\n        user = self.require_user()\\n        if not user:\\n            return\\n        params = {\\\"client_id\\\": config.CLIENT_ID, \\\"redirect_uri\\\": config.REDIRECT_URI,\\n                  \\\"scope\\\": config.SCOPES, \\\"state\\\": sign_state(user[\\\"id\\\"])}\\n        self.redirect(AUTHORIZE_URL + \\\"?\\\" + urllib.parse.urlencode(params, safe=\\\":,\\\"))\",\"new_string\":\"    # Step 1: build the authorize URL. The SPA fetches it (so the sign-in\\n    # token travels in the Authorization header) and then points the browser\\n    # at it; a plain link/navigation cannot carry that header.\\n    def auth_init(self):\\n        user = self.require_user()\\n        if not user:\\n            return\\n        params = {\\\"client_id\\\": config.CLIENT_ID, \\\"redirect_uri\\\": config.REDIRECT_URI,\\n                  \\\"response_type\\\": \\\"code\\\", \\\"scope\\\": config.SCOPES, \\\"state\\\": sign_state(user[\\\"id\\\"])}\\n        url = AUTHORIZE_URL + \\\"?\\\" + urllib.parse.urlencode(params, safe=\\\":,\\\")\\n        self.send_json(200, {\\\"url\\\": url})\"}"}}]}
